data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done","summary_index":0,"item_id":"rs_02179466161470600000000000000000000ffffac1508844bb3aa","output_index":0,"text":"Compile pass. Now simulation. Need design test cases. Tool schema cases list with steps inputs, expected, optional dt_ms. Need understand expected format. We can create handful. Need infer cases structure: \"cases\": [{\"name\": ..., \"steps\":[{\"inputs\": {...}, \"expected\": {...}, \"dt_ms\": ...}]}]. Need maybe time values strings. The tool likely accepts. We need test initial? Maybe expected output after each step. Use cases with EStop priority, debounce, runtime preserve, maintenance not tested (500h too long maybe can simulate large CycleTime? CycleTime TIME max 4294967295ms = 49.7 days, need ~10 scans to 500h, possible. But debounce at each invocation uses CycleTime too, so with DebounceTime small qualified quickly. Could add 500h via CycleTime large but runtime 500h, cycle max ~49 days, 10 steps. But maintenance informational. Yet formal verify P6. We can test threshold via cycle times? Since a single cycle less than 500h, after run true and accumulate. We can set DebounceTime 1ms, CycleTime max 4294967295ms (close 49.7d), first run false, then each run adds 49.7d. 11 cycles crosses 500h (~20.8d? Wait 500h = 20.83 days, below TIME max 49.7 days!). 500 hours is 20.833 days, so a single cycle could exceed threshold! CycleTime max 49.7 days. Starting after debounce qualification with huge CycleTime: scan k0 permit true, DebounceTime 1ms, run false, acc 0, elapsed += 49.7d. k1: elapsed >= 1ms qualified run true, acc += 49.7d >500h -> maint true. Great. Maintenance threshold can be tested in 2 scans.\n\nBut simulation tool dt_ms maybe must be integer and inputs TIME as strings. Use common. Need expected maybe exact outputs. We can include case 1:\nCase \"debounce_and_runtime\":\n steps:\n0 dt 100 inputs VISCOSITY_OK true, ESTOP true, DebounceTime T#200ms, CycleTime T#100ms expected VFD_RUN false, PUMP_MAINT_DUE false, PUMP_RUNTIME_ACC \"LTIME#0ns\"\n1 same dt100 expected VFD_RUN false, acc 0\n2 expected VFD_RUN true, acc 100ms (LTIME#100ms) because run at end of scan based elapsed at beginning =200ms and accumulate 100ms. Then debounceElapsed? after qualified no advance. Good.\n3 expected run true acc 200ms maybe. Then case input visc false dt100 expected false, acc 200ms (no accumulation).\n4 re-enable true with DebounceTime 200: first sample elapsed reset-> run false, acc still 200. \n5 expected false\n6 expected true acc 300ms? Let's map: after step4 (visc false) reset. step5 visc true: elapsed 0 false. step6 visc true: elapsed 100 false. step7 visc true: elapsed 200 true acc +=100. So more.\n\nAlternative each case fresh. We can test e-stop priority/reset and debounce re-qualification.\n\nNeed know `dt_ms` semantics. Step dt 100. CycleTime inputs maybe use `T#100ms` or `\"100ms\"`? Interface expects TIME. Tool likely maps strings. Examples unknown. Could just send `DebounceTime`: \"T#200ms\", `CycleTime`: \"T#100ms\", dt_ms: 100. Expected outputs bool and `\"LTIME#0ns\"`. Need exact. The simulator may parse CODESYS literal. We can try. Max 3 simulation calls; don't waste, structure carefully.\n\nCould use a single simulation call with multiple cases. Each call can include many cases. Let's do 3 cases:\n1 debounce timing and runtime.\n2 E-stop immediate stop and new full debounce.\n3 viscosity false immediate reset and 500h threshold? Can't combine 500h with small debounce? We can do case 3 maintenance threshold with huge cycle.\n\nNeed expected outputs carefully. Let's simulate semantics.\n\nCase 1 steps:\n- Inputs all steps same VISCOSITY_OK true, ESTOP true, DebounceTime 200ms, CycleTime100ms.\nstep0 dt100:\n initial debounceElapsed 0, qualified false. Compare 0>=200 false. run false. acc 0. maint false. then elapsed += 100.\nexpected acc LTIME#0ns.\nstep1 dt100:\n elapsed100 false. run false. acc0. elapsed200.\nstep2:\n elapsed200 >=200 -> qualified true, run true, acc +=100, maint false. expected acc LTIME#100ms. elapsed not advanced because qualified.\nstep3:\n run true acc 200ms.\nThis confirms.\n\nCase 2 E-stop:\ninputs Step0 maybe ESTOP true visc true Debounce 100ms Cycle100ms -> false (elapsed100 after).\nstep1 permit true -> elapsed100 qualify run true acc100.\nstep2 same -> run true acc200.\nstep3 ESTOP false -> run false, acc preserved 200, elapsed reset 0, qual false.\nstep4 ESTOP true -> first requal false, acc200.\nstep5 -> second requal run true acc300.\nNeed if step3 CycleTime 100 not accumulated. yes. step4 no run, debounce elapsed resets? After step3 at !permit sets elapsed 0. step4 permitted and qualified false; elapsed 0 compare 0>=100 false, then +=100. Step5 elapsed100 -> run. yes.\n\nCase 3 viscosity false + maintenance threshold:\nCould test maintenance threshold with DebounceTime 1ms, CycleTime maybe 1000h? TIME max 4294967295ms, 500h = 1,800,000,000ms, which fits TIME. Great! We can set CycleTime T#500h? Is TIME literal T#500h allowed? TIME range max 4294967295 ms (~1193h), 500h fits. Use CycleTime T#500h, DebounceTime T#1ms. Step0: elapsed0 false, acc0. Step1: elapsed500h >=1ms qualify, run true, acc +=500h => PUMP_MAINT_DUE true. Expected acc LTIME#500h. This tests threshold. But also note runtime accumulation exactly 500h. Great.\nCould also test that maintenance due doesn't stop run; step2 same -> run true, maint true, acc 1000h.\nCase 3 addresses viscosity? maybe no. Another case can test viscosity false priority, but max cases per call unlimited. Add case 4 small. One simulation call can include 4 cases. Good.\n\nCase 4 viscosity loss:\nStep0 permit true Debounce 100, Cycle100 -> false (elapsed100)\nstep1 permit true -> qualify run true acc100\nstep2 VISC false -> run false, acc100, debounce cleared\nstep3 VISC true -> false acc100 (elapsed100 after)\nstep4 VISC true -> true acc200\nCould verify.\n\nNeed step0 dt always? If omitted default? We'll include `dt_ms`. Inputs each step need all four. Expected maybe full outputs each. Need maybe optional expected keys only outputs. We'll include all.\n\nLet's call simulate_st with current source.","sequence_number":642}

